stth wrote:
> Doug Freyburger wrote:
>
>  > With peaceful mines I still want the objects of the peaceful
>  > monsters so I count kills.  Four hostiles per peaceful ...
>
> Doesn't that approach tend to leave quite a few peacefuls behind on
> each level? There simply aren't enough hostiles (that 4:1 ratio) in
> my experience.

On either type of mines I will do some amount of competing with my pet
for kills for my experience levels and some amount of guiding my pet to
kills for its experience levels.  In a peaceful mine my pet levels up
more but I often decide to descend before my pet takes them all out.

> Leaving a potential mithril-coat behind can make all the difference.
> That's why I generally want their items as soon as possible and the
> "let your pet kill them" approach manages that nicely.

Since only dwarves and elves will have mithril I make sure to not leave
any of those behind.  In a peaceful mine I could easily ignore several
other types of peacefuls.

>  > Having learned that difference in strategy it stopped being an
>  > advantage or disadvantage.  Just a call for different strategies.
>
> My only problem with hostile mines is wand-zapping monsters.

About as often I go down a trap door and end up in a dark area not
knowing where the up stairs is.  It's very easy to get too surrounded by
a crowd of hostiles when that happens.

A few times I get killed by attacking a dwarf who has a mattock.  That
hasn't happened to me in a while.  If I have damage I tend to use my pet
to find that out first.

I still lose a lot of pets to the watch captain's weapon.  Not my
character but it is the same principle as my character getting killed by
a dwarf with a mattock.  Pets use unarmed monster level to decide if
they should attack.  They don't count the high damage of a very good
weapon like a long sword.  Pets don't use any strategy in crowds and
mine tend to attack the watch captain when there walls on monsters in
the way of retreat.

I try to walk my pet around individual watchmen to take them out one at
a time.  It usually works and it does clear the field for eventually
killing the watch captain on the way back up.

Not taking pet strength into account - One of my first knights to get to
mine town lost his horse to the priest.  I have no idea that horses were
that much more powerful than large dogs and the combat was over by the
time I could maneuver between them and start herding my horse out of the
shrine.  It only took a couple of blows to go from healthy horse to dead.


